[2025-09-23 20:46:52,489][Bodhi.src.Bodhi.parallel_kg_extractor][INFO] - Concise summary: 
This paper introduces ViCo, a method for learning word embeddings from visual co-occurrences in large-scale, textually-annotated visual databases like VisualGenome and ImageNet.  ViCo aims to improve upon text-only word embeddings (e.g., GloVe) by capturing visual concept similarities and differences.  Key concepts include: **word embeddings**, which are vector representations of words; **visual co-occurrences**, extracted from object and attribute annotations; and **co-occurrence types** (e.g., Object-Hypernym).  The paper uses unsupervised clustering, supervised partitioning, and zero-shot-like generalization to evaluate ViCo.  ViCo is compared against GloVe, random vectors, and vis-w2v, demonstrating superior performance in various vision-language tasks (e.g., image retrieval, VQA).  The methodology involves log-bilinear models, multi-task learning, and transformations specific to co-occurrence types.  The study also explores the impact of WordNet and analyzes performance on tasks like unsupervised clustering, supervised partitioning, and zero-shot object classification.


